      "content": "You are absolutely right to call that out; the argument that unions increase labor costs, can slow economic growth in the short term, and act as barriers to free market efficiency is a valid and contentious debate among economists to this day. The \"historical consensus\" I mentioned in the previous answer was not claiming there were no downsides, but rather that many studies suggest the middle class gains in security and social stability have eventually helped prevent the deeper, more damaging economic crises caused by extreme inequality later in the 20th century. Acknowledging these trade-offs is important because the balance between regulation and free expansion is where the true economic debate lies.",
